ilton.com (brand booking; not fetched)","commission_status":"commissioned","commission":"hotel","tier_absent_reason":"not-established","commission_pct_low":10,"commission_pct_high":10,"commission_basis":"published","wells":["stay"],"draw_rank":26,"blurb":"Listed by HotelGuides at \"7775 I-40 East, Amarillo, TX 79118\" (I-40, Exit 75), \"18 miles\" from Palo Duro Canyon State Park. Hilton''s own page was not fetched, so nothing else is stated here."},{"name":"Panhandle-Plains Historical Museum","source":"https://www.panhandleplains.org/","accessed":"2026-10-03","si":["culture","family","senior"],"booking_route":"own website / 806-651-2244 (admission not read)","commission_status":"unknown","commission":"venue","tier_absent_reason":"does-not-compete","wells":["activities"],"draw_rank":27,"blurb":"2503 4th Avenue, Canyon, Texas 79015 - collections spanning art, archaeology, geology, paleontology, textiles, transportation and regional history. 806-651-2244. Hours and admission were not on the page read."},{"name":"Cadillac Ranch","source":"https://www.visitamarillo.com/things-to-do/route-66/","accessed":"2026-10-03","si":["motoring","culture","family"],"booking_route":"none needed (no fee read)","commission_status":"none","tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","wells":["activities"],"draw_rank":28,"blurb":"\"Now located along I-40 in Amarillo, Cadillac Ranch and the Big Texan Steak Ranch and Microbrewery are two famous Route 66 attractions\"; Visit Amarillo invites visitors to \"spray your mark on Cadillac Ranch\"."},{"name":"Amarillo Route 66 Historic District","source":"https://www.visitamarillo.com/things-to-do/route-66/","accessed":"2026-10-03","si":["motoring","culture","culinary"],"booking_route":"none needed - a public street district","commission_status":"none","tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","wells":["activities","shop"],"draw_rank":29,"blurb":"\"The official Route 66 Historic District in Amarillo is located on 6th Ave. between Georgia & Western Streets\"; \"Route 66 is turning 100, and there''s no better place to celebrate than Amarillo.\""},{"name":"The Big Texan Steak Ranch","source":"https://www.bigtexan.com/","accessed":"2026-10-03","si":["culinary","family","motoring"],"booking_route":"walk-in (no booking method read)","commission_status":"unknown","commission":"venue","tier_absent_reason":"does-not-compete","wells":["eat"],"draw_rank":30,"blurb":"\"Feeding folks since 1960\", family-owned: \"The Big Texan''s legendary 72-ounce steak challenge is not for the faint of heart\" - finish it within one hour and it is free; attempts are live-streamed. Visit Amarillo calls it a Route 66 attraction now on I-40."},{"name":"GoldenLight Cafe and Cantina","source":"https://www.goldenlightcafe.com/","accessed":"2026-10-03","si":["culinary","entertainment","motoring"],"booking_route":"walk-in (no booking method read)","commission_status":"unknown","commission":"venue","tier_absent_reason":"does-not-compete","wells":["eat"],"draw_rank":31,"blurb":"2906 SW 6th Ave, Amarillo, in the Route 66 district: a restaurant and music venue hosting \"local and touring bands\"; Visit Amarillo calls it \"Amarillo''s oldest restaurant, offering up live music and great burgers\"."},{"name":"Tyler''s Barbeque","source":"https://www.tylersbarbeque.com/","accessed":"2026-10-03","si":["culinary","family"],"booking_route":"walk-in","commission_status":"unknown","commission":"venue","tier_absent_reason":"does-not-compete","wells":["eat"],"draw_rank":32,"blurb":"3301 Olsen Boulevard, Amarillo: \"Real Texas barbeque. Oak-smoked daily. Served until it''s gone.\" Tuesday-Friday 11 AM-7 PM, Saturday 11 AM-6 PM, closed Sunday and Monday."},{"name":"OHMS Cafe & Bar","source":"https://www.ohmscafe.com/","accessed":"2026-10-03","si":["culinary","romance","wine"],"booking_route":"not read","commission_status":"unknown","commission":"venue","tier_absent_reason":"does-not-compete","wells":["eat"],"draw_rank":33,"blurb":"619 S. Tyler St., Amarillo: \"Amarillo''s Upscale American Bistro\" - \"Nestled in the historic downtown of Amarillo, OHMS Cafe & Bar fuses the soul of Santa Fe with the heart of the panhandle.\" Lunch Tue-Fri, dinner Tue-Sat."},{"name":"Bracero''s Mexican Bar & Grill","source":"https://www.visitamarillo.com/restaurants/","accessed":"2026-10-03","si":["culinary","motoring"],"booking_route":"not read","commission_status":"unknown","commission":"venue","tier_absent_reason":"does-not-compete","wells":["eat"],"draw_rank":34,"blurb":"Visit Amarillo: located in what was once a Route 66 gas station in the Historic District."},{"name":"Rain Premier Sushi Bar and Lounge","source":"https://www.visitamarillo.com/restaurants/","accessed":"2026-10-03","si":["culinary","romance"],"booking_route":"not read","commission_status":"unknown","commission":"venue","tier_absent_reason":"does-not-compete","wells":["eat"],"draw_rank":35,"blurb":"Visit Amarillo: \"A popular downtown Amarillo restaurant\" with a cosmopolitan feel."},{"name":"Public House","source":"https://www.visitamarillo.com/restaurants/","accessed":"2026-10-03","si":["culinary","family"],"booking_route":"not read","commission_status":"unknown","commission":"venue","tier_absent_reason":"does-not-compete","wells":["eat"],"draw_rank":36,"blurb":"Visit Amarillo: elevates comfort food to a new level."},{"name":"Palo Duro Canyon group camps and Mack Dick Pavilion","source":"https://tpwd.texas.gov/state-parks/palo-duro-canyon/fees-facilities/group-facilities-","accessed":"2026-10-03","si":["group","family","senior"],"booking_route":"Texas State Parks Customer Service Center by phone, (512) 389-8920","commission_status":"unknown","commission":"hotel","tier_absent_reason":"not-established","wells":["stay"],"draw_rank":37,"blurb":"TPWD''s group sites inside the park: \"Group Camp (Wolfberry)\" - nine campsites for up to 72 people with \"30/50-amp electric and water hookups\", $225 nightly; a youth \"Group Camp\" for 32 people, $50 nightly; the Tasajillo Pavilion for 54, $300 daily; and the \"Group Hall with Kitchen (Mack Dick Pavilion)\" for 169, air-conditioned and wheelchair accessible, $1000 daily or \"$2000 total\" Friday-Sunday - all plus entrance fees. \"Call (512) 389-8920 for reservations\"."}],"faq":[{"q":"How much is it to get into Palo Duro Canyon State Park?","a":"TPWD lists \"Adult (13+ years): $8\" daily and \"Child (12 years and under): Free\". The $70 Texas State Parks Pass gives \"unlimited visits to more than 80 state parks without paying entrance fees\".","source":"https://tpwd.texas.gov/state-parks/palo-duro-canyon/fees-facilities/entrance-fees","accessed":"2026-10-03"},{"q":"Do you need reservations for Palo Duro Canyon State Park?","a":"TPWD recommends you \"Check day pass availability and make reservations online\"; the park \"often reaches capacity\". \"Day pass entrance permits may be reserved up to one month before your visit\", and \"Day passes are especially important for weekends and holidays and at our busiest parks.\"","source":"https://tpwd.texas.gov/state-parks/park-reservation-information/faq","accessed":"2026-10-03"},{"q":"Can you camp at Palo Duro Canyon State Park?","a":"Yes: water-and-electric sites at $26 (Juniper, Mesquite/Sagebrush, Hackberry, up to 60-foot RVs on two loops), tent-only drive-up sites at $16, and hike-in primitive camping at $12 (\"Minimum 1/2 mile hike; bring your own water\"), all plus entrance fee, booked online through ReserveAmerica.","source":"https://tpwd.texas.gov/state-parks/palo-duro-canyon/fees-facilities/campsites","accessed":"2026-10-03"},{"q":"Does Palo Duro Canyon State Park have cabins?","a":"Yes: three CCC-built rim cabins - Lighthouse and Goodnight at $125 a night and Sorenson at $150, each sleeping 4 - and four Cow Camp cabins on the canyon floor at $60, where \"Restrooms with showers are nearby\" and \"No linens provided\". Glamping is booked separately: \"you cannot reserve this site through our system.\"","source":"https://tpwd.texas.gov/state-parks/palo-duro-canyon/fees-facilities/cabins","accessed":"2026-10-03"},{"q":"Does Palo Duro Canyon State Park allow dogs?","a":"Yes, on a leash: \"Keep pets on leashes for their safety, and to protect wildlife.\" Bring extra water for them - TPWD notes that on the Lighthouse Trail \"most heat-related injuries and deaths for people and pets occur\". Pets are not allowed in the cabins.","source":"https://tpwd.texas.gov/state-parks/palo-duro-canyon/trails-info","accessed":"2026-10-03"},{"q":"What time does Palo Duro Canyon State Park close?","a":"TPWD gives gate hours of \"7:30 a.m. - 10 p.m.\" and office hours of \"7:30 a.m. - 5:30 p.m.\", open daily.","source":"https://tpwd.texas.gov/state-parks/palo-duro-canyon","accessed":"2026-10-03"},{"q":"Where is Palo Duro Canyon State Park?","a":"\"The second largest canyon in the country lies in the heart of the Texas Panhandle.\" The park address is 11450 Park Road 5, Canyon, TX 79015; (806) 488-2227.","source":"https://tpwd.texas.gov/state-parks/palo-duro-canyon","accessed":"2026-10-03"},{"q":"What is there to do in Palo Duro Canyon State Park?","a":"TPWD lists hiking, biking, horseback riding, camping, geocaching, bird watching and \"summer performances of the TEXAS Outdoor Musical\", across \"over 30 miles of trails\" and 1,500 acres for riding.","source":"https://tpwd.texas.gov/state-parks/palo-duro-canyon","accessed":"2026-10-03"},{"q":"Where should I stay near Palo Duro Canyon State Park?","a":"HotelGuides lists the nearest hotels in Canyon, 14 miles away - Hampton Inn & Suites Canyon and Holiday Inn Express & Suites Canyon at I-27 Exit 106 - with more on I-40 in Amarillo at 18-20 miles. Inside the park there are cabins, campsites and Palo Duro Glamping.","source":"https://hotelguides.com/attraction-maps/texas/map-hotels-near-palo-duro-canyon-state-park-tx.html","accessed":"2026-10-03"}],"seo":{"meta_title":"Palo Duro Canyon State Park: Lighthouse Trail, Cabins & Camping | TravelWell","meta_description":"The second largest canyon in the US: the Lighthouse Trail, CCC rim cabins, campsites and glamping, the TEXAS Outdoor Musical, and where to stay nearby.","slug":"palo-duro-canyon-state-park-texas","primary_keyword":"Palo Duro Canyon State Park","jsonld_type":"TouristDestination"},"hero":{"query":"Palo Duro Canyon Lighthouse rock formation"}}'::jsonb, 68),
  ('grand-canyon-arizona', '12A', 'Grand Canyon National Park', 'United States', 'A mile-deep canyon seen from a paved rim, a train from Williams, a river run of up to 18 days, a ring of tented camps, and a park mid-recovery from the August 2026 flood', 'future', 'verified', 'mountainValley', 'The Southwest Desert', '{"hiking","adventure","glamping","family","culture","parks"}', '{"dramatic","rugged","historic","adventurous"}', '{}', null, null, '{"safety":{"posture":"book-freely","source":"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/usa","verified":"2026-10-08"},"jewels":[{"name":"The Rim Trail, Mather Point to Yavapai Point","source":"https://www.nps.gov/places/rim-trail-mather-point.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["hiking"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"the most-used 0.7-mile stretch of the South Rim, 15-30 minutes one way with constant canyon views, ending at the 1928 Yavapai Geology Museum on the rim edge"},{"name":"Hermit Road (Red Route)","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/shuttle-buses.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["family"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"a 7-mile rim road of overlooks, closed to private vehicles 1 March - 30 November 2026 and served by a shuttle every 12 minutes until an hour after sunset"},{"name":"Desert View Watchtower","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/desert-view.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["culture"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Mary Colter''s tower modelled on ancestral Puebloan architecture, 23 miles east of Grand Canyon Village at the end of Desert View Drive"},{"name":"Bright Angel Trail day hikes","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/key-messages.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["hiking"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"graded turnaround points at Mile-and-a-Half and Three-Mile resthouses; as of 28 September 2026 the trail is open to River Resthouse but resthouse water is turned off"},{"name":"South Rim mule rides","source":"https://www.grandcanyonlodges.com/alerts/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["adventure"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Xanterra-run rides, currently on a limited schedule: the Canyon Vista Mule Ride (8:00 and 12:00) and the Havasupai Garden Mule Ride (7:00)"},{"name":"Colorado River raft trips","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/whitewater-rafting.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["adventure"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"professionally guided trips of 3 to 18 days, often reserved a year or two ahead, run by 15 NPS-authorised concessioners; longer private trips go through a weighted lottery"},{"name":"Grand Canyon Star Party","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/grand-canyon-star-party.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["family"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"a free week of telescopes on the South Rim each June (6-13 June 2026), with nightly talks at 8 pm and constellation tours from 9 pm"},{"name":"The North Rim","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/northrimstatus.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["expedition"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"open through 30 November 2026 or first major snow, with campground, general store and scenic drives; no overnight lodging since the Dragon Bravo Fire destroyed Grand Canyon Lodge"},{"name":"El Tovar and the Historic District lodges","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/lodging.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["culture"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"El Tovar Hotel, Bright Angel, Kachina, Thunderbird and Maswik lodges, run by Xanterra on the rim; closed to overnight guests through 31 October 2026"},{"name":"Under Canvas Grand Canyon","source":"https://www.undercanvas.com/camps/grand-canyon/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["glamping"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Grand Canyon Junction (Valle): safari-style tents with ensuite baths and wood stoves, including glass-roofed Stargazer tents, on a DarkSky-certified 160-acre site about 25 minutes from the South Rim entrance; open 15 April - 26 October 2026"},{"name":"Clear Sky Resorts: Grand Canyon","source":"https://grandcanyon.clearskyresorts.com/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["glamping"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Valle: transparent sky domes for 2 to 7 guests, from standard to themed domes, with stargazing tours, yoga and Native American flute-making; closes for the 2026 season on 17 October"},{"name":"Wander Camp Grand Canyon","source":"https://thewandercamp.com/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["glamping"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Valle area, about 25 minutes from the South Rim: off-grid canvas bell tents of 200+ sq ft with shared bathrooms, solar lighting, communal fire pits and an on-site food truck"},{"name":"WYLDSTAY Grand Canyon","source":"https://www.wyldstaygrandcanyon.com/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["glamping"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"40 private acres about 25 minutes outside the South Rim: an off-grid tented ''luxury dark sky resort'' with a holistic spa, yoga, sound baths and a labyrinth"},{"name":"Highland Grand Canyon","source":"https://highlandgrandcanyon.com/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["glamping"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Rowe Well Road, about 4 miles from the South Rim: five boutique off-grid tents on 10 acres in the Kaibab National Forest, locally owned, with an outdoor kitchen, fire pit and telescope"},{"name":"Backland Luxury Nature Resort","source":"https://grandcanyon.com/news/grand-canyon-glamping-guide/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["glamping"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"a luxury glamping resort listed about 90 minutes from the South Rim; unit types and amenities were not verified in this build"},{"name":"Grand Canyon Glamping Resort (Grand Canyon West)","source":"https://www.explorekingman.com/attraction-grand-canyon-glamping-resort/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["glamping"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"glamping wagons, tipis, tents and Stargazer Domes plus dry camping sites, a 1-mile drive from the Grand Canyon West Skywalk entrance"},{"name":"Shash Diné Eco-Retreat","source":"https://www.shashdine.com/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["glamping"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"12 miles south of Page: a year-round glamping stay on a working ranch raising Navajo Churro sheep, built around a Navajo cultural experience"},{"name":"Village Camp Flagstaff","source":"https://villagecamp.com/resorts/flagstaff/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["glamping"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Flagstaff: an outdoor cabin and RV resort with rental cabins, clubhouse bar and bistro, seasonal pool and year-round hot tub"},{"name":"Mather Campground","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/camping.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["family"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"the NPS campground in Grand Canyon Village, open all year, no hookups, with laundry and showers; reservations up to 6 months ahead via recreation.gov; currently dry camping under Stage 4 water restrictions"},{"name":"Desert View Campground","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/desert-view.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["adventure"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"NPS campground 23 miles east of the Village, open 11 April - 18 October 2026, no hookups, bookable up to six months ahead on recreation.gov"},{"name":"North Rim Campground","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/camping.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["adventure"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"the NPS campground on the North Rim, open 1 June - 15 October 2026; water and RV hookups not available; reservations via recreation.gov"},{"name":"Tusayan - Montane Campground (formerly Ten-X)","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/camping.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["adventure"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Kaibab National Forest campground 9 miles south of the Village, open 15 May - 10 October 2026, no hookups"},{"name":"Grand Canyon Camper Village","source":"https://home.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/cg-sr.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["family"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"private campground in Tusayan, 7 miles south of Grand Canyon Village, open seasonally, with hookups and coin-operated showers"},{"name":"DeMotte Campground","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/camping.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["adventure"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"National Forest campground 7 miles north of the North Rim park boundary, no hookups; its status after the 2025 Dragon Bravo Fire was not established"},{"name":"Kaibab Camper Village","source":"https://www.campendium.com/kaibab-camper-village","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["family"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Jacob Lake, about 40 miles from the North Rim: 60 gravel sites, open May - October, listed as the only campground near the North Rim with RV hookups"},{"name":"Grand Canyon KOA (Highway 64)","source":"https://experiencewilliams.com/rv-parks-in-williams-arizona-where-to-hook-up/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["family"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"5333 State Highway 64, described as the closest RV park in Williams to the Grand Canyon, with 50-amp sites, a pool and fire-pit rentals; open year-round"},{"name":"Williams / Circle Pines KOA","source":"https://experiencewilliams.com/rv-parks-in-williams-arizona-where-to-hook-up/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["family"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"4 miles west of downtown Williams: sites to 90 feet, 50-amp, pool, spa, mini golf and bike rentals; open 15 March - 1 November"},{"name":"Grand Canyon Railway RV Park","source":"https://experiencewilliams.com/rv-parks-in-williams-arizona-where-to-hook-up/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["family"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"beside the Williams train depot, the only all-paved RV park in Williams, with pull-through, buddy and back-in spaces, pool, hot tub and a pet resort"},{"name":"Canyon Motel & RV Park","source":"https://experiencewilliams.com/rv-parks-in-williams-arizona-where-to-hook-up/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["family"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Williams, beside the Kaibab National Forest: historic motel rooms, caboose suites and railway-car suites alongside large-rig RV sites"},{"name":"Tusayan","source":"https://www.mygrandcanyonpark.com/road-trips/gateway-towns/tusayan/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["family"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"the closest town to the South Rim, about a mile from the park gate, with hotels, tours and the Grand Canyon National Park Airport for scenic flights; its lodging is unaffected by the in-park closure"},{"name":"Grand Canyon Railway from Williams","source":"https://experiencewilliams.com/listing/grand-canyon-railway/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["family"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"a heritage train running since 1901 from the Williams depot to the South Rim, about two and a half hours each way, with dining and observation cars; reported operating in late September 2026"},{"name":"Bearizona Wildlife Park","source":"https://bearizona.com/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["wildlife"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Williams: a drive-through of more than three miles of ponderosa forest past bears, wolves, bison and elk, plus the walk-through Fort Bearizona; open daily 9 am - 4 pm"},{"name":"Grand Canyon Brewing + Distillery","source":"https://grandcanyonbrewery.com/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["culture"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"a Williams brewery and distillery with a taproom at 301 North 7th Street, making beer, spirits and ready-to-drink cocktails"},{"name":"Flagstaff and Lowell Observatory","source":"https://www.flagstaffarizona.org/things-to-do/astrotourism/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["culture"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"the world''s first International Dark Sky City (24 October 2001), home of the observatory known for the discovery of Pluto"},{"name":"Grand Canyon West Skywalk","source":"https://grandcanyonwest.com/things-to-do/skywalk/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["culture"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"a glass walkway 70 feet out over the rim at Eagle Point on the Hualapai Reservation, about 4,000 feet above the canyon floor; ticketed and not affiliated with the national park"},{"name":"Grand Canyon Trail Rides","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/mule_trips.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["family"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"formerly Apache Stables, just outside the South Entrance near Tusayan: one- and two-hour Kaibab National Forest rides plus twilight campfire and wagon rides"},{"name":"Canyon Adventures Bike Tours and Café","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/bicycling.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["family"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"beside the Grand Canyon Visitor Center: bike rentals and guided rides (mid-March to 31 October) on about 13 miles of rim roads and Greenway trails, including a 6.5-mile paved run to Tusayan"},{"name":"South Rim coach tours (Xanterra)","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/guidedtours.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["senior"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"daily in-park coach tours to Hermits Rest and/or Desert View, plus sunrise and sunset tours"},{"name":"Grand Canyon Conservancy Field Institute","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/guidedtours.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["hiking"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"the park partner''s guided programme, from a family day hike to a backpacking trip or an outdoor photography class"},{"name":"Papillon helicopter tours","source":"https://www.papillon.com/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["adventure"],"tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"helicopter flights from the South Rim, including the 25-30 minute North Canyon Tour and the 40-50 minute Grand Kingdom; air tours operate outside park boundaries"},{"name":"El Tovar Dining Room, South Rim","source":"https://www.grandcanyonlodges.com/dining/el-tovar-dining-room-and-lounge/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["culinary","romance","culture","senior"],"booking_route":"Tock (exploretock.com), parties of 1-10 up to 60 days ahead; dinner reservations required","tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Native stone and Oregon pine dining room in the historic El Tovar Hotel with murals of Hopi, Apache, Mojave and Navajo customs; Prime Rib Hash and Salmon Tostada served for decades. The concessioner page currently lists all services as closed."},{"name":"Rod''s Steak House, Williams","source":"https://experiencewilliams.com/listing/rods-steak-house/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["culinary","culture","family","senior"],"booking_route":"phone (928) 635-2671","tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Family-owned Route 66 steakhouse operating since 1946, keeping a 1940s steak grill and its neon cow awning, at 301 E Historic Route 66; per the Williams visitor bureau listing."},{"name":"Pine Country Restaurant, Williams","source":"https://www.pinecountryrestaurant.com/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["culinary","family","senior"],"booking_route":"walk-in; online ordering for takeout; phone 928-635-9718","tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Gateway diner known for pies made daily in more than 50 flavours, from Lemon Meringue to Chocolate Covered Cherry Cream; open 7 am-9 pm."},{"name":"Cruiser''s Cafe 66, Williams","source":"https://experiencewilliams.com/listing/cruisers-cafe-66/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["culinary","culture","family","group"],"booking_route":"not observed","tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Route 66 cafe at 233 W Route 66 with restored gas pumps and road signs, a patio on the Mother Road, summer live music, and locally crafted beers and spirits; per the Williams visitor bureau listing."},{"name":"Flagstaff Brewery Trail","source":"https://www.flagstaffarizona.org/dining-nightlife/breweries/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["culinary","group","culture"],"booking_route":"own form (LoyalBrew digital passport registration) or printed passport","tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Free passport across Flagstaff''s breweries (digital via LoyalBrew or printed); collect every sticker, with no time limit, for a commemorative pint glass at the Flagstaff Visitor Center."},{"name":"Beaver Street Brewery, Flagstaff","source":"https://www.flagstaffarizona.org/directory/beaver-street-brewery-breweries/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["culinary","group","family"],"booking_route":"not observed","tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Flagstaff''s first brewpub, opened March 1994, still brewing in its original brewhouse with wood-fired pizzas and the 21+ Brews & Cues billiards bar; per the Flagstaff visitor bureau listing."},{"name":"Historic Barrel House, downtown Flagstaff","source":"https://www.flagstaffarizona.org/directory/historic-barrel-house-flagstaff-breweries/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["culinary","group"],"booking_route":"not observed","tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Historic Brewing Company''s microbrewery and gastropub at 110 S. San Francisco Street, a stop on the Brewery Trail; per the Flagstaff visitor bureau listing."},{"name":"Dark Sky Brewing Taproom & Pizzicletta, Flagstaff","source":"https://www.flagstaffarizona.org/directory/dark-sky-brewing-taproom-pizzicletta-breweries/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["culinary","group","romance"],"booking_route":"not observed","tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Microbrewery and pizzeria pairing at 117 N Beaver Street in downtown Flagstaff, a stop on the Brewery Trail; per the Flagstaff visitor bureau listing."},{"name":"Drinking Horn Meadery Mead Hall, Flagstaff","source":"https://www.drinkinghornmeadery.com/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["wine","culinary","culture"],"booking_route":"walk-in; phone (928) 266-0425","tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Honey-mead producer (''Live Simply. Drink Simply.'') with a Mead Hall tasting room at 108 E Route 66."},{"name":"Brandy''s Proper Cafe, Flagstaff","source":"https://www.flagstaffarizona.org/directory/brandys-proper-cafe/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["culinary","family","culture"],"booking_route":"not observed","tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Route 66 cafe joining Brandy''s Restaurant & Bakery (50-year history) with Proper Meats + Provisions'' James Beard-nominated butcher-driven sandwiches; per the Flagstaff visitor bureau listing."},{"name":"Atria, Flagstaff","source":"https://www.flagstaffarizona.org/directory/atria/","accessed":"2026-10-02","si":["culinary","romance","wine"],"booking_route":"not observed (phone (928) 440-5280 listed)","tier_absent_reason":"not-sold","blurb":"Chef Rochelle Daniel''s seasonal modern American restaurant at 103 N. Leroux St using locally grown ingredients; she is a James Beard Award semifinalist and 2017 Arizona Culinary Hall of Fame inductee; per the Flagstaff visitor bureau listing."}],"faq":[{"q":"Can you stay overnight inside Grand Canyon National Park right now?","a":"Not in park lodging until November. After the 29 August 2026 flash flood damaged the park''s water line, all in-park hotels closed to overnight guests from 31 August, and the closure has been extended through 31 October 2026. Reservations arriving 1 November and later are proceeding as planned, pending water restoration. Hotels in Tusayan, outside the park, are unaffected.","source":"https://www.grandcanyonlodges.com/alerts/","accessed":"2026-10-02"},{"q":"Where can you go glamping near the Grand Canyon?","a":"Most of the camps sit in the Valle / Grand Canyon Junction corridor south of the South Rim. Under Canvas Grand Canyon is about 25 minutes from the South Rim entrance and runs 15 April - 26 October 2026; Clear Sky Resorts, Wander Camp and WYLDSTAY are in the same area, and Highland Grand Canyon is about 4 miles from the rim.","source":"https://www.undercanvas.com/camps/grand-canyon/","accessed":"2026-10-02"},{"q":"Do I need a permit to hike in the Grand Canyon?","a":"Not for a day hike. The NPS says a backcountry permit is required for overnight camping outside Mather, Desert View and North Rim campgrounds, and that day hikes, including nighttime day hikes, need none. Organised non-commercial groups of 12-30 doing rim-to-rim need a Special Use Permit.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/backcountry-permit.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02"},{"q":"How do you reserve a campsite at the Grand Canyon?","a":"NPS campground reservations can be made up to six months ahead through recreation.gov or by phone at (877) 444-6777. NPS campgrounds in the park have no RV hookups, and Trailer Village, the full-hookup RV park, is currently closed due to water shortage.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/camping.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02"},{"q":"How much does it cost to enter Grand Canyon National Park?","a":"$35 per private vehicle, $30 per motorcycle, or $20 per person on foot or bicycle, valid for 7 days. Non-US residents aged 16 and over pay an extra $100 each unless they hold an Annual or America the Beautiful pass.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/fees.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02"},{"q":"Is the North Rim open in 2026?","a":"Yes, through 30 November 2026 or the first major snowstorm, with the campground, general store and select scenic drives and viewpoints open. There is no overnight lodging on the North Rim since the Dragon Bravo Fire destroyed Grand Canyon Lodge, and the North Kaibab Trail is closed.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/grca/northrimstatus.htm","accessed":"2026-10-02"}],"seo":{"meta_title":"Grand Canyon National Park: South Rim, Glamping & River | TravelWell","meta_description":"What is open at the Grand Canyon after the August 2026 flood, the glamping camps and RV parks around it, and how the river trips, mules and Williams train work.","slug":"grand-canyon-national-park-arizona","primary_keyword":"Grand Canyon National Park","jsonld_type":"TouristDestination"},"hero":{"query":"Grand Canyon South Rim, Arizona"}}'::jsonb, 69)
